   the card on the front page: ything, and use your own words. Balancing marks on a clock face: how few are needed? A theorem, checked by machine Put marks on the hours of a clock, and ask in which ways they balance. How few marks can balance in exactly a chosen set of ways? For clocks of pⁿ·q hours, when an hour may be marked more than once, there is a complete answer, with a proof that a proof checker has accepted: a small theorem about the balances that mathematicians use when they ask which sets of marks tile a clock. It settles nothing about tilings. The page says how it was found, how the statement was tested apart from the checker, and what it does not show. No person has read it. How far to trust these pages No pe
   files note on the files page: The paper and its source, the two Lean files with the statements, the referee's three reports on the paper, the audit of the Lean statements, the readings of this page and of its later sections, the readings of it by two visitors on a small screen, and the page as it was before them.
   README.before-visit: kept as ['files/README.before-visit.md'] ; in the old build: ['files/README.before-visit.html', 'files/README.before-visit.md']

